# Commands the docs name on purpose because they do NOT exist. Each line is a
# deliberate mention; deleting the line would make the checker demand the
# sentence be removed, and the sentence is the useful part.

# docs/boot-v2.md: "There is deliberately no `rime boot ack` verb: the notice is
# cleared automatically on a boot where no entry is exhausted, so it tracks
# reality instead of tracking whether somebody dismissed it."
rime boot ack

# docs/recovery.md: "Adding an `rime recover previous` verb would have been a
# second name for an operation that already exists" (namely `sudo rime rollback`).
rime recover previous

# docs/rollback.md: "An earlier version of this document claimed
# `rime reset --keep-home` shipped in M3. It never did; the verb above is what
# exists." Naming the retired claim is how a reader who saw the old doc knows.
rime reset

# docs/fleet.md is a DESIGN, and it says so in its first two sentences: "No code
# here exists. There is no `rime fleet` verb, no enrolment record, no server."
# The verb names are the shape being proposed, and a checker that demanded they
# be removed would turn a design document into a document that cannot describe
# what it is designing. They leave this file on the day the verb ships.
rime fleet
rime fleet join
rime fleet leave
rime fleet status
# The design's own test-shaped guarantee: "the client must be able to print
# exactly what it would send without sending it, the way `rime channel report`
# does today. `rime fleet report --dry-run` is that verb."
rime fleet report


# docs/rime-owns-its-esp.md opens with Andre's decision in his own words:
# "windows side install should be like everything else with the systemd-boot.
# maybe it should build a new esp for rime or something." That is a quotation
# of SPEECH, not a claim that a verb exists — but `mentions()` scans prose, not
# just backticked text, so it reads "rime or something" as a two-word verb and
# refuses it. roadmap/v2.2 went red on exactly this the moment the doc landed.
#
# The line is here rather than in the document because the document's whole
# value is that the decision is recorded verbatim; rewording Andre to satisfy a
# regex would destroy the only thing it is for. The extractor's real limitation
# is that it cannot tell an English function word from a subcommand name —
# nothing is or will be named `or` — and tightening it is a separate change
# with its own both-ways test, not something to smuggle in behind a doc fix.
rime or something

# docs/nixos-move.md: `rime migrate` passes every argument unchanged to the
# bridge program /usr/libexec/rime-migrate (Python, argparse), so its
# subcommands are not clap's and `rime migrate check --help` can only answer
# where that program is installed, which a CI runner is not. They are real
# commands: tests/test-rime-migrate.sh asks the shipped program for each one's
# --help and fails if any does not parse.
rime migrate check
rime migrate repair-e
rime migrate key-export
rime migrate start

# docs/nixos-move.md names the step that gives back the space the old system
# keeps after a move: "a step (`rime migrate finish`) that is planned and not in
# this release". It is the NixOS plan's reclaim step (PLAN.md D2), named so a
# user knows the space is not gone for good. It leaves this file the day the
# verb ships.
rime migrate finish
